<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Tuvalu</h1><p class="st-meta">1978 CE – present · capital Funafuti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pacific atoll nation of about 10,000 people, independent from the United Kingdom since 1978, vulnerable to rising seas and a constitutional monarchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> were part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a> colony until a 1974 referendum led to separate administrations, in 1975 and 1976. Tuvalu became fully independent within the Commonwealth on 1 October 1978, with Toaripi Lauti as its first prime minister.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tuvalu is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> and Commonwealth realm, with the King represented by a governor general. The unicameral Parliament has 16 members, who choose the prime minister, and there are no formal political parties. A 2008 referendum on becoming a republic kept the monarchy. Tuvalu joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 5 September 2000. Its economy rests on fishing licences, grants and aid, and remittances from seafarers, and it has been active in climate negotiations as a low-lying state.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The Constitution of Tuvalu Act 2023 took effect on 1 October 2023 and recognised the Falekaupule as the traditional governing authorities of the islands. On 10 November 2023 Tuvalu signed the Falepili Union treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, which provides a pathway for 280 citizens to migrate to Australia each year. Charles III is King of Tuvalu.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tuvalu.jpg" alt="Tuvalu" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tuvalu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1978 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Funafuti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Toaripi Lauti, Enele Sopoaga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christian (Congregational Christian Church of Tuvalu, the state church), Baháʼí Faith</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuvaluan, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Tuvalu</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ellice Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=tuvalu" aria-label="Tuvalu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9629 2495 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tuvalu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9949 2751L9806 2386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9806 2386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9949 2751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9949 2751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Funafuti. <a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=tuvalu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
